2 The 40-Year Purist Guarantee
******************************

Every stock GNU Emacs keychord works exactly as documented in the
official GNU Emacs manual:
   • ‘C-x C-f’ finds files, ‘C-x b’ switches buffers, ‘C-s’ searches.
   • ‘M-x’ executes commands, ‘C-g’ cancels, ‘C-h’ provides help.
   • ‘TAB’ indents, ‘C-M-i’ invokes completion-at-point.
   • ‘C-x 2’, ‘C-x 3’, ‘C-x 0’, ‘C-x 1’, ‘C-x o’ manage windows.
   • ‘C-x t’ manages tabs and workspaces.

   Custom Hell Emacs commands live only under ‘C-c’, the prefix reserved
by GNU Emacs conventions for users, and only where stock Emacs has no
key: the groups never repeat ‘C-x C-f’, ‘M-.’, ‘C-x p c’ and the like,
and nothing is added inside Emacs' own ‘C-x’, ‘M-g’ or ‘M-s’ prefixes.
Installed packages keep their own default keys as they ship them:
Magit's ‘C-x g’, lsp-mode's ‘s-l’, diff-hl's ‘C-x v’ hunk keys.
‘C-c h’
     Hell Emacs core commands (‘C-c h s’ Altar, ‘C-c h k’ IntelliJ
     Finder, ‘C-c h i’ Info manual, ‘C-c h p’ Relic Chamber, ‘C-c h r’
     Crucible).
‘C-c c’
     Code commands a language server adds (‘C-c c a’ code actions, ‘C-c
     c r’ rename, ‘C-c c o’ organize imports, ‘C-c c f’ format).
‘C-c l’
     The Localleader: mode-specific commands (Java refactorings, test
     runners).
‘C-c d’
     Debugging (‘C-c d d’ start, ‘C-c d b’ breakpoint).
‘C-c s’
     Search (‘C-c s s’ buffer lines, ‘C-c s p’ the project).
‘C-c t’
     Toggles (‘C-c t l’ line numbers, ‘C-c t f’ flymake).


