4 Daily JVM Workflow
********************

Hell Emacs provides deep language support, debugging, and building for
the complete JVM ecosystem.

4.1 Java (‘:lang java’)
=======================

Powered by Eclipse JDTLS, java-debug, and Spring Boot Tools:
   • *Code Completion*: Semantic member completion, type hints, Lombok
     annotations.
   • *Refactoring & Code Actions*: Press ‘C-c c a’ for quick fixes and
     intentions.  Rename across the project with ‘C-c c r’.
   • *Navigation*: ‘M-.’ jumps to definition, ‘M-?’ finds all usages
     across the project, and ‘M-,’ returns.
   • *Spring Boot*: Bean navigation, application properties completion,
     '‘@Autowired’' cross-references.
   • *Testing*: Run the JUnit test at point (‘C-c l t t’) or the test
     class (‘C-c l t T’); results on ‘C-c l t r’.

4.2 Debugging with DAP (‘:tools debugger’)
==========================================

Native Debug Adapter Protocol integration via ‘dap-mode’ and Eclipse
‘java-debug’:
‘C-c d d’
     Start a new debugging session (‘dap-debug’).
‘C-c d b’
     Toggle breakpoint on current line (‘dap-breakpoint-toggle’).
‘C-c d n’
     Step Over next instruction (‘dap-next’).
‘C-c d i’
     Step Into method call (‘dap-step-in’).
‘C-c d o’
     Step Out of current method (‘dap-step-out’).
‘C-c d c’
     Continue execution until next breakpoint (‘dap-continue’).
‘C-c d r’
     Restart current debug frame (‘dap-restart-frame’).
‘C-c d q’
     Disconnect and stop debugging session (‘dap-disconnect’).
‘C-c d E’
     Evaluate expression in current execution frame (‘dap-eval’).

4.3 Build & Test Systems (‘:tools build’ & ‘:tools test’)
=========================================================

Direct invocation of project wrappers and tools without leaving Emacs:
‘C-x p c’
     Build the project with its Gradle or Maven wrapper
     (‘project-compile’).
‘C-c l t t’
     Run the test at point.
‘C-c l t T’
     Run the test class.
‘C-c l t r’
     Show the test results.
‘C-c r r’
     Execute project Run Configuration (‘.hell-emacs/run.eld’,
     ‘.run/*.xml’).

4.4 JDK Discovery & Multi-Version Management
============================================

Hell Emacs automatically locates every JDK installed on the machine:
   • Scans SDKMAN! (‘~/.sdkman/candidates/java/*’), system paths
     (‘/usr/lib/jvm/*’), Mise, ASDF, and ‘JAVA_HOME’.
   • Runtimes are mapped directly to JDTLS, allowing different projects
     to target Java 8, 17, 21, or 25 simultaneously while the language
     server executes on modern Java.

4.5 Kotlin, Clojure, Groovy, and Scala
======================================

   • *Kotlin* (‘:lang kotlin’): Kotlin Language Server with Gradle/Maven
     support.
   • *Clojure* (‘:lang clojure’): CIDER REPL integration with
     ‘clojure-lsp’.
   • *Groovy* (‘:lang groovy’): Groovy Language Server built from pinned
     Gradle verification.
   • *Scala* (‘:lang scala’): Metals language server and sbt build tool
     integration.

4.6 Interactive Help & Shortcuts Hub
====================================

Access the interactive JVM Help and Cheatsheet Hub at any time:
     M-x hell-help
   Shortcut: ‘C-c h h’.  Displays live language server status, debugger
readiness, discovered JDK paths, and full keybinding tables.


